hw/sh4: ze0 2DG glyph blit sets the destination alpha bit for AUI text (M8.C1v).

On the map screen the AUI blits its button labels into keyed DU plane 1
(mode 0x1002, key 0x03ff) as 0xa8xxxxxx alpha glyphs from auirtdll.dll
(emitter fn 0x03d07b20, build B 0x03cd0000). The last UI display list
0x0e8d4c70 targeting plane 1 at 0x0ef6c400 contains 52 such 0xa8003100
glyphs (colours 0x6f7b / 0x1084 / 0x7fff). ze0-0014's glyph() blends the
glyph colour onto the destination but preserves dst&0x8000, so every label
pixel is written with bit 15 clear and C-8502's colour key drops it: L4
screen shows only the 1,502 already-opaque widget pixels, no labels.

Here the glyph() writes the blended colour with the alpha (opacity) bit
set, so the text survives the 0x03ff key in the composed screen and the
map buttons (Map Menu / ZOOM OUT / 1-16mi / ZOOM IN) are visible over the
map. Map-plane (0x4002) glyphs are unaffected: their plane is not keyed and
the alpha bit is ignored on those pixels. Negative control: with this
single OR disabled, the label pixels have bit 15 clear again and the key
drops them (EXP-2026-11-24-M8.C1v).

Signed-off-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

--- a/hw/sh4/ze0_2dg.c
+++ b/hw/sh4/ze0_2dg.c
@@ -271,6 +271,7 @@ static void glyph(Dl *d, uint16_t c, uint32_t wh, uint32_t pos)
             uint16_t *p = &px[x - xa];
 
             if (a) {
-                *p = cpu_to_le16(blend(le16_to_cpu(*p), c, a));
+                /* AUI glyphs are opaque (C-8506, M8.C1v): set alpha bit. */
+                *p = cpu_to_le16(blend(le16_to_cpu(*p), c, a) | 0x8000);
             }
         }
         address_space_write(&address_space_memory,
